    "abstract": "The gut-brain axis is increasingly recognized as a critical mediator of mental health, with fermented dairy products emerging as potential modulators of this relationship through their influence on gut microbiota composition. A TLR4-deficient murine model revealed altered responses to dietary probiotic supplementation, suggesting that innate immune signaling pathways may regulate the microbiota's capacity to influence neurobehavioral outcomes. 16S rRNA sequencing demonstrated that fermented dairy-fed mice exhibited distinct microbial community structures compared to controls, with increased abundance of Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium species. These changes correlated with reduced anxiety-like behaviors in behavioral assays, indicating a potential mechanistic link between microbiota shifts and mood regulation. Further, the TLR4 mutation was associated with diminished production of short-chain fatty acids, key metabolites implicated in gut-brain communication. These findings suggest that dietary interventions targeting specific microbial taxa may offer therapeutic potential for depressive disorders by modulating immune-related gene expression and metabolic output of the gut. The interplay between host genetics and microbiota-driven neuroactive metabolites highlights the complexity of the gut-brain axis and its relevance to mental health.",
