    "abstract": "The gut-brain axis is increasingly recognized as a critical mediator in the pathogenesis of major depressive disorder, with emerging evidence pointing to the role of gut microbiota in modulating neuroinflammation and neurotransmitter production. tryptophan hydroxylase 2 (Tph2), a key enzyme in serotonin synthesis, was found to be significantly downregulated in the brain of depressed patients compared to healthy controls, suggesting a potential link between gut microbial metabolites and central serotonergic function. To explore this further, we analyzed fecal samples from 120 participants using 16S rRNA sequencing and identified a significant depletion of Prevotella in depressed individuals. A multivariate analysis of variance revealed that Prevotella abundance correlated inversely with Tph2 expression levels, indicating a possible microbial influence on central serotonin metabolism. Notably, supplementation with the probiotic Lactobacillus rhamnosus led to a partial restoration of Tph2 expression and improved mood scores in a subset of patients with major depressive disorder. These findings support the hypothesis that gut microbial dysbiosis contributes to neurochemical imbalances and that targeted microbiome modulation may offer a novel therapeutic approach for mood disorders.",
