    "abstract": "A multivariate analysis of variance revealed that the administration of a probiotic blend containing Lactobacillus rhamnosus and Bifidobacterium longum significantly altered the gut microbiome composition in male and female C57BL/6J mice following a 12-week intervention. These changes were associated with improved behavioral outcomes in a colitis-induced anxiety model, as measured by open field and elevated plus maze tests. The principal component analysis highlighted a distinct clustering of microbial taxa, including increased abundance of Allobaculum and Odoribacter, which correlated with reduced inflammatory cytokine levels in serum. Notably, the permutation-based multivariate analysis demonstrated that the probiotic treatment modulated the gut-brain axis by enhancing the production of short-chain fatty acids, particularly butyrate, which in turn influenced hippocampal neuroplasticity markers. These findings suggest that targeted modulation of the gut microbiome through probiotic supplementation could represent a viable strategy for mitigating gut-related psychiatric comorbidities and improving cognitive function in individuals with chronic gastrointestinal disorders.",
