    "abstract": "The dysregulation of the gut microbiota has been increasingly linked to inflammatory bowel disease and its associated neurological comorbidities, including depression. Using 16S rRNA sequencing and metabolomic profiling, we analyzed fecal samples from conventional C57BL/6J mice fed a standard chow diet or a diet supplemented with fermented soybeans, which are rich in short-chain fatty acids. Mice on the fermented soybean-enriched diet exhibited a significant increase in the abundance of the Lactobacillus genus and a concomitant decrease in pro-inflammatory lipopolysaccharide levels in the colonic mucosa. Notably, the Tlr4 expression was downregulated in these animals, correlating with reduced inflammation and improved gastrointestinal barrier function. Furthermore, the fermented soybean-diet group showed enhanced behavioral tests outcomes, suggesting a potential role for microbiota-modulating diets in alleviating both gut and brain-related pathologies. These findings highlight the interplay between gut microbiota, immune-related genes, and dietary interventions in modulating inflammatory and neurobehavioral disorders.",
