    "abstract": "The TLR4 gene has been implicated in modulating immune responses and inflammation, which are key components of the gut-brain axis. Recent single-cell RNA sequencing has revealed distinct transcriptional profiles of immune cells in the gut mucosa that correlate with neuroinflammatory markers in the brain. These findings suggest a potential mechanistic link between intestinal immune activation and cognitive decline in Alzheimer's disease. To explore this further, metabolomic profiling was employed to assess the fecal microbiota-derived metabolites in patients with Alzheimer's disease and age-matched controls. A significant enrichment of short-chain fatty acids, particularly butyrate, was observed in the control group, suggesting a dysregulated microbiome in Alzheimer's disease patients. Quantitative PCR confirmed a reduced abundance of Faecalibacterium prausnitzii, a known butyrate producer, in Alzheimer's disease subjects. These results highlight the role of the gut microbiome in modulating neuroinflammation through immune signaling pathways involving the TLR4 gene. The integration of single-cell RNA sequencing with metabolomic and microbial profiling provides a comprehensive view of how immune-microbial interactions might influence disease progression in Alzheimer's disease.",
